the panels', 'Turn this pedal on or off for this track', 'Drag to move the drums', 'Computer keys follow the Studio window', 'Repeat the drum chart each bar', 'Clear the drum chart', 'Play this drum', 'Assign a computer key to this drum', 'Mute this drum', 'Click to place a beat, hold Shift for an accent, right-click to set its velocity', 'Choose a drum kit', 'Connect a MIDI keyboard to the drums', 'Choose a MIDI input for the drums', 'Click the ruler to move the playhead while stopped or playing', 'Choose this track\'s colour',  'Mute this channel', 'Solo this channel', 'Choose the export format', 'Choose the export bit rate', 'Include the mastering rack', 'Export the whole song as one mixed audio file', 'Choose a drum loop', 'Save the drum pattern as a loop', 'Load the chosen drum loop', 'Delete the chosen drum loop', 'Add a bar to the drum loop', 'Remove a bar from the drum loop', 'Add a beat to the drum loop', 'Remove a beat from the drum loop', 'Name this drum loop', 'Open a saved studio song', 'Drag clips and their edges', 'Select a time range across tracks', 'Click a clip to cut it at that point', 'Repeat this time range', 'Print this range through each track\'s pedals', 'Export this range as a file', 'Clear the selection', 'Close this list', 'Open this song' ],
		summary => 'A multitrack audio studio with takes and a pedalboard.',
		verified => '2026-11-24',
		body => [
			'Studio is a multitrack recorder and editor: takes, crossfades, waveform clips, and a pedalboard routed onto individual channels.',
			'The knobs and pedal backdrops follow the selected icon set, so the mixer wears the same finish as the rest of the desk.',
			'Every track has a compact panel on its left, inside the row: the arm button cycles Off, Record, Play and Loop, the slider sets that track against the others, FX selects the track and opens the pedalboard where its chain lives - the panel itself never lists effects, so it keeps the same height whatever the track has in its chain; Auto opens its automation lane, the name is editable, the colour swatch paints that track\'s recordings, and Delete removes the track. These controls stay even when the channel strips are hidden, so a narrow window loses width rather than the controls. New tracks are given a colour of their own, drawn from the theme so light and dark both read; the choice is saved with the song. Drag a track\'s bottom edge to make its row taller or shorter: every track keeps its own height, saved with the song, and the timeline scrolls beneath it while the automation lane keeps its own height. FX, Auto and Delete sit on their own line under the volume, so the panel\'s controls never reach across into the canvas.',
			'A song starts with one track. Add track adds another up to 32, the engine\'s own limit; Delete asks twice before it removes a track. Loading a saved song brings back the tracks it was saved with.',
			'The layout bar carries the panels\' taskbar: one entry for the transport, the track listing, the channel strips and the drums. New, Open, Save and Configure sit together at the left edge of the bar: Open lists every saved studio song in a dialog, and picking one loads it. Every panel is an interior window of the Studio desk, and its title bar has Minimize and Maximize buttons. The pin beside them keeps that panel above the others; press it again to release it, and a pinned panel stays on top however the rest of the stack is raised. Minimizing hides the window but leaves its work running - a take, the metronome and the keys still play - and its taskbar entry brings it back. The taskbar entry for the panel at the front minimizes it; any other entry raises that panel. The grip at the start of the layout bar moves the whole Studio window, so a crowded title bar is never the only way to drag it. The choice is remembered on the next visit.',
			'Studio only paints the tracks and channel strips you can see. When the window is minimized, or Configure covers the timeline, the transport and the meters keep running but nothing is redrawn until the window is back. A track scrolled out of the timeline, or a strip scrolled out of the mixer, is painted again as soon as it comes into view.',
			'Every panel is an interior window of its own: drag its title bar to move it anywhere over the Studio desk, and drag the corner grip to resize it. Windows stack inside Studio, and a press on one brings it to the front. Maximize fills the desk with that window and puts it back in its own box when pressed again; Minimize sends it to the taskbar. Each window\'s place, size, minimized state and maximized state are remembered with the song. A window never grows past the room the desk has, and its content scrolls inside it. Arrange puts every window back in the DAW reading order in one press. The bottom edge resizes the window\'s height and the corner its width and height together, above a track\'s own bottom grip, so the panel can always be made taller without hunting for the corner.',
			'The taskbar also has a right-click menu: Tile all lays the windows out in the reading order, Cascade steps them down the desk, and Minimize all (or Restore all) sends every window to the taskbar or brings them back. With Studio in front, Ctrl+Alt+1 to Ctrl+Alt+4 raise the transport, track listing, channel strips or drums; Ctrl+Alt+0 minimizes or restores every panel, Ctrl+Alt+T tiles and Ctrl+Alt+C cascades.',
			'The channel strips fill the console\'s width at a floor of 76 pixels each, so there is no empty gap before Master, and they still scroll inside their own window, so every fader is reachable even when the window is short, and many strips still scroll sideways. Each strip carries its input jack and its output cord side by side at the head, so the source and the destination are both in reach: the jack chooses what feeds the track and the cord what the track feeds. Each strip carries its track\'s volume fader beside its knobs: the fader and the slider under the track are the same control, so moving either moves both. Every panel window is its own scroll box, so one panel\'s content can never cover another; the master rack is the strips\' last strip on the right, a compact block that wraps and scrolls in place. A channel\'s input or output can name another channel: choose Channel 1 as Channel 2\'s input (or Channel 2 as Channel 1\'s output) and the first channel\'s post-pedal output feeds the second channel\'s tone stack and pedals, where Monitor or a take picks it up. The two jacks show the same edge, and reopening either one highlights the same row. The chosen row wears a steady theme tint and border rather than a flashing alarm colour.',
			'Arrange, beside the panel taskbar, lays the windows out the way a DAW does in one press: the transport is a single row across the top, only as tall as its controls, the track timeline takes the large middle, the drum machine sits beside it, and the mixing console sits in a compact band at the foot, its master strip at the far right of the strips. A narrow window stacks the same areas in one column instead of squeezing them. Arrange brings every window back from minimized or maximized and remembers the new places.',
			'The jog wheel shares the transport\'s button row, so the panel\'s width sets the room and the wheel never covers Previous, Next or the 4/4 box.',
			'The window\'s full-screen button - behind the app icon, beside the pin - fills the whole screen with Studio, over the dock and the taskbar; move the pointer to the top right corner for the button that brings the window back where it was.',
			'The Pedals button in the layout bar opens the pedalboard as a sheet over the foot of the window: it floats above the console, so opening it never shrinks the track timeline, and Close in its own bar returns the console. Its height is bounded to the window: the Add list and the selected track\'s pedalboard live in the sheet, and the board scrolls sideways in place. A channel starts with an empty board; choose an effect from the Add list at the top and press Add. The transport\'s burger does the same when the transport is tall enough to show it. The Latency row shows what the browser reports for this session: the total output figure, its base and output parts in milliseconds, and the sample rate in hertz.',
			'Every pedal tile on the board wears a small lamp. Green means that effect is on for the selected track; dark red means it is bypassed or not in that track\'s chain. Press the lamp (or the tile\'s footswitch) and the pedal switches in place exactly as the chain does: an effect the track does not have yet is added to its chain, and the next press bypasses it. The lamp reads the chain, so the two can never disagree. Jtune\'s lamp is its tuner light instead: amber while it listens and green when the note is in tune.',
			'Every tile also carries a footswitch: it reads On or Off, and pressing it switches that effect for the selected track exactly as the lamp does.',
			'A track\'s chain is a rack, and the pedalboard is the one place it is drawn. Pick the track with its panel or its strip, then open the pedalboard with its Pedals button or the track\'s FX button: the Add list at the top of the sheet offers every effect, and Add puts the chosen one in the chain the In/Out switch shows. The board under the bar shows that track\'s inserts as stompbox tiles in signal order: the first insert hears the tone stack, the last one feeds the fader. Choosing another track with its panel or its strip redraws the board for that track. The Add track button sits at the end of the layout bar.',
			'Every insert is a stompbox tile on the board, with its artwork or drawn body, its controls, a footswitch marked On (or Off), and small Up, Down and X tools. The footswitch bypasses the insert where it stands and leaves it in the chain with its settings; press it again and it is back. X removes it. Drag a tile to put it somewhere else in the chain, or use Up and Down when a finger is easier than a drag. The tile you drag is drawn above every panel, and a dashed marker shows where it will land. The board shows one track\'s chain at a time - the selected track\'s - so reorder within it; to put an effect on another track, select that track and add it from the Add list. Either move changes the audio chain and the saved order. The order and the on/off state are saved with the song.',
			'A ruler sits over the timeline: click it to move the playhead to that moment while the transport is stopped or playing; a recording keeps its place. It follows the transport\'s display, so it reads bars and beats or seconds. The Tracks title bar above the ruler carries three tools: Cursor moves and trims clips, Select draws a time range across one or more tracks, and Cut splits a take at the point you click. Right-click a recording on a track for Split, Cut, Copy, Paste, Delete and Bounce: Split cuts it at the point you clicked, Cut takes it to the clipboard, Copy leaves it where it is, Paste drops a copy at the point you right-clicked on that track, and Delete removes it. Bounce prints that track\'s Out pedals into the take and marks it printed, so the pedals stop costing CPU on it and it plays dry; a Split or a copy of a printed take stays printed. Drag a recording along its track to move when it plays, or drag it onto another track to move it there. Each recording is drawn in its track\'s colour.',
			'A track\'s rack has two positions. In is before the recording, so the pedals print into the take and the file is wet; Out is after the take, so a clean recording plays through the pedals when you listen and when it is exported. The In and Out switch at the head of the board chooses which chain Add puts an effect into and which chain the tiles on the board belong to, and each chain keeps its own order, switches and settings. The Drums entry in a channel\'s Input list patches the kit into that channel: the kit is heard through the channel\'s output - its fader, pan, Out pedals and output cord - and its own dry feed is muted while patched. The In chain is still what a take records; press Monitor on an app to hear the In chain, the signal the take will contain, instead of the output path. Synth, Music and Marker follow the same rule: the app is live through the channel output while it is patched, and Monitor swaps to the In chain. A monitored microphone or instrument also joins the channel output, so its fader, pan and output cord apply; when the native engine\'s Listen owns the device, its direct engine output is the monitor instead.',
			'The channel fader takes most of each strip, and M and S under it mute that channel or solo it against the rest; solo dims every strip that is not soloed. The transport\'s buttons are larger, its jog wheel is twice their size, and every dial is drawn as a roundel.',
			'The drum loop can be lengthened by a bar or a beat: Add bar and Remove bar change it by sixteen steps, Add beat and Remove beat by four. Save loop keeps the pattern under a name in the drum loop library; Load brings one back and Delete removes it. Right-click a loop track for Add drum loop, which places the saved pattern on that track as a looping clip at the point you clicked.',
			'With Select, the range you draw shows a bar under the Tracks title bar: Loop repeats it (the ruler selection sets the same span), Bounce prints that span through each selected track\'s Out pedals into one printed take per track, Export renders the span across the selected tracks as WAV, MP3 or FLAC using the format and bit rate in Configure, and Clear puts the range away. A range drawn on the ruler applies to every track; a range drawn on a lane applies to the lanes it crosses.',
			'Configure carries Export: choose WAV, MP3 or FLAC, a bit rate, and whether the mastering rack is included, then press Export mix. The whole song is rendered once through every track\'s output pedals and fader and the mastering rack, then saved on the song as one audio file named after the song, where the song\'s Files list can open or download it. A printed take is mixed dry there, because its pedals are already in its audio. Stereo Widener sits in the pedalboard and the master rack; the native engine take does not process it yet.',
			'Drums and keys: the drum machine is a panel window of its own with eight synthesised voices - Kick, Snare, Closed Hat, Open Hat, Clap, Low Tom, High Tom and Rim - over one bar of sixteen steps. A Kit list beside Loop chooses the whole set of voices - Acoustic, Electronic, Techno, Beats, Latin, Pearl, Tama, Gretsch, 808 and Metal - while every voice keeps its row, key and step; the set is saved with the song. Click a step to place a beat; hold Shift and click for an accent, which is a harder hit. Right-click a placed beat for its velocity menu: minus, the value and plus, from a soft tap to a full accent. The step fades with its velocity. The lamp on each voice mutes it, Clear empties the chart, and a cleared chart is a valid song. Arrange puts the drums in a window of their own beside the timeline; minimize that window and its taskbar entry brings it back. The panel scrolls inside itself, so a short window never steals the timeline\'s height.',
			'The chart follows the transport: Play or Record starts it, the tempo comes from the BPM box, and Loop repeats the bar each time. With Loop off it plays once and the rest of the song carries on. Every voice has a computer key, shown on its row: click the key button, then press a new key to move it, or Escape to cancel. Keys only sound while no text field has focus, no take is selected and Studio is the window you last touched; the panel\'s bar says which state it is in, so typing a track name never plays a kick. Where the browser offers Web MIDI, Connect MIDI lists the inputs and a note plays its voice, with velocity choosing the accent; where it does not, the panel says so plainly. The drums are heard through the master rack, and the browser take records them on the selected track - the track you arm. The native engine take cannot record them yet, and the panel says that too.',
			'Every effect is added from the Add list at the top of the pedalboard. Newer effects - Flanger, Fuzz, Amp, Limiter, JawnPitch, Equaliser and Tone among them - have no hand-drawn artwork of their own, so the pedalboard draws them in the theme\'s body: a title plate, a real knob for each control with its name under it, a dropdown for a whole-number choice such as the cabinet, the key or the scale, and the same footswitch. They wear the drawn body on the chain row too. JawnPitch follows one note at a time and pulls it to the key and scale you choose, so it suits a voice or a single line; a chord confuses it.',
			'The Jtune pedal is a chromatic tuner, not an audio effect: it is a listen-only pedal in a track\'s rack, and it goes wherever the rack puts it. Add it from the Add list, then use Up or the rack drag to put it first and it hears the clean input; after a pedal it hears that pedal. Its switch is the pedal\'s On/Off: off, it is bypassed, the signal still passes through untouched and the tuner stops listening. Its screen names the nearest note and the cents it is off by: the arrow on the left means tune up, the arrow on the right means tune down, and the lamp goes from grey (off) to amber (listening) to green when the note is within five cents. Point the track at the interface\'s input (arm it or press its Monitor) and the tuner hears the guitar or bass on that input; it opens no microphone of its own, and if the track has no live input it says so. The tuner follows the selected track: click another strip and it listens to that track\'s tuner. It changes nothing in the sound and the native engine\'s take leaves it out. The song keeps it in the chain like any pedal.',
			'Reverb has five models, chosen from the dropdown on its shelf pedal: Room, Spring, Plate, Hall and Cave, from a small bright room to a very long low cave. The dropdown moves every reverb at once, like the pedal\'s knobs; a rack row keeps the model it was left with.',
			'The master rack is the last of the channel strips, at their far right, on the mixer\'s own bus after every track: Compressor, Equaliser, Reverb, Maximiser and Limiter, in that order. Each has a footswitch and its own controls, and all of them start off, so the mix sounds the same until you switch one on. The chain is fixed - it is the house\'s mastering order, not a per-song list - and the song remembers which ones were on and where their controls were left. An engine take arms only the mastering effects that are on when it starts; its footswitch can bypass or restore one of those while it records, but switching on an effect that was off then is heard on the next take.',
			'The Round trip row\'s backend picker chooses who measures: this browser plays a few clicks through the speakers and times the echo on the microphone, while ALSA, PipeWire or JACK asks the audio engine on this machine and shows its number, or its error when there is no device. When a native engine owns the interface, that measured round trip is also the take\'s record offset, so a take lands on the metronome; the click itself is never shifted for output latency.',
			'When the audio engine is running on this machine, it owns the transport clock, the metronome and the takes\' audio: Play, Stop and a click on the ruler drive the engine in seconds, and the browser playhead and the takes follow the position it reports, so one bar phase and one click serve the whole transport. Each armed take is exported as a WAV on this machine and the engine plays it on its own sample clock, so the browser schedules no second copy; moving, trimming, splitting, deleting or adding a take while it plays is heard at the next block, and so is a fader, pan, Out pedal, master knob or master insert change, because the engine swaps the plan without closing the output (a master knob moves its value only, so the rack and its tails are kept). Each take plays through its track\'s Out pedals and the enabled master rack, so the engine mix sounds like the browser\'s, and a take the engine cannot take (a video, or one that is not ready) keeps the whole mix in the browser instead. Its click sits on the song\'s own beat grid: the first beat after wherever you start is a real beat of the song (the downbeat, or the beat you started in the middle of), and the first beat of each absolute bar is accented. A loop wraps the position at its end back to its start and reads the grid again from there, so the loop\'s downbeat stays a downbeat. Changing the tempo, signature, metronome switch, metronome volume or loop retunes the engine in place; it never closes the output. An engine take may record while the transport plays the existing takes: the take captures on its own stream while the transport keeps the output, so one stop ends both, and the take can monitor its live input beside the transport. The transport still owns the click, so a take\'s own monitor files or its own click need the transport stopped first. When the engine is not running, or refuses because a take\'s monitor, a playback, listen or the patchbay owns the output, Studio falls back to the browser clock, click and take audio, and the browser places no second click while the engine owns it. The browser metronome still runs about two seconds ahead, so a busy window cannot make it stumble; a beat already behind the clock is skipped and the beats after it keep their places.',
			'An Engine take block sits under Round trip: Start take and Stop take drive the native audio engine instead of the browser, reading Seconds and one to eight Tracks. Each engine track has its own Input picker, listing Input 1 up to the number of inputs the interface has, and a Source picker: Mono reads that one channel, while Stereo also reads the next channel and halves each, so the last input can only be Mono. Each engine track records through that track\'s own rack, so the pedals you see on channel 1, 2 and so on are the chain the engine arms; only the effects switched on are sent. A channel send on the jacks is armed too: a track whose Input jack names another channel records that source track\'s take signal - its trim and its In pedals, placed in send order - instead of an interface channel, so Channel 2\'s input can be Channel 1\'s output. The Out chain is browser playback only, so its pedals are not in the engine take. The Listen inserts box belongs to Listen, not the take. One row per track shows the take\'s input and source (for example "in 1 · mono"), its state, its frames and its dropped count, with Gain (0 to 4) and Pan (-1 to 1) sliders that move that track\'s strip while it records; whatever the engine answers is shown as it sent it. With the metronome on, an engine take makes its own click on the engine\'s output clock, using the tempo, the signature\'s top number and the metronome\'s volume, with the first beat of each bar accented. The browser places no second click while the take records, so the click and the take share one sample clock. The click is heard in the monitor and is never written into the take\'s file; a take with no monitor still opens the output for its click. Count-in, beside Tracks, uses the Count-in setting from Configure: when the engine transport is stopped, the browser clicks the count-in even with the metronome off, then the engine transport and the take start together on the punch-in beat, so the song starts on its own beat 1. With the transport already playing there is no browser count-in; the take starts at the playhead. While the transport owns the output, the take records without a click of its own, because the transport\'s click is the metronome, but it can still monitor its live input beside it.',
			'Listen, below those controls, starts live monitoring on the engine without recording: choose Input 1 or Input 2 (the interface\'s two channels), set the Listen gain, and press Listen again (it then reads Listening) to stop. The selected track\'s pedals are the monitor\'s chain, in order. Each track\'s Monitor button hears only what that track\'s input jack feeds, through the pedals (headphones only, or the speakers will feed back); one track is monitored at a time, and pressing another track\'s Monitor moves it there. With no input chosen it is silent and opens no microphone. With Synth, Drums, Music or Marker it plays that app through the channel output in the browser and opens no microphone either; while the app is patched the channel fader, Out pedals and output cord are its sound, and Monitor swaps it to the In chain. With a microphone or instrument, Monitor starts the engine\'s live Listen on the input you chose in the jack\'s list (Input 1 is channel 0 and Input 2 is channel 1; on the UM2 the instrument input is channel 1) - through the selected track\'s pedals, and stops it when Monitor goes off; on a machine without the engine, or when the engine cannot start, the browser capture is the monitor instead, and the sentence beside the button says so. A guitar on the interface\'s instrument input is heard in both ears while it monitors. An engine take takes that choice when it starts; while it records, pressing Monitor silences or restores the live input it armed, so the take keeps running either way. Pressing Monitor while it records starts the live input from that moment, and pressing it again silences it; the recording so far is unaffected. While it listens the row shows the engine\'s state, the chosen input and its frame count, and the gain slider moves the monitor level. Only one capture is available at a time, so listening and recording exclude each other. On a phone the app\'s own audio engine is the monitor: it opens the microphone itself, reports the sharing mode, rate, buffer and xrun count Android granted, and follows the phone\'s media volume, so the hardware keys set the level.',
			'It works with the same library as Music, so recorded and imported tracks sit together.',
			'Each microphone or interface row in the jack\'s list carries its own Input 1/Input 2 picker, so the channel can take either of the interface\'s two inputs. A channel\'s input is a plug you choose: a microphone or camera, Marker\'s whiteboard, Music\'s #video, another window or screen share, or the Synth — pick Synth and what you play records through the channel\'s tone stack and pedals like any other source. Marker and Music record their own picture and sound; screen share offers the desktop app\'s screens and windows in a list, and records the one you pick, with its audio when the platform offers it.',
			'This machine\'s input, under that list, names which of the machine\'s own capture sources the engine records when the track\'s input is a real device: the built-in microphone, the audio interface, a USB microphone. The list leaves out a speaker\'s own monitor, because that is playback rather than something you can speak into, and it shows each source by its name. The machine\'s default input is a row of its own, and it is what a channel uses until you pick another; the choice is saved with the house plan, so the engine captures that source and the picker opens on it next time. A source that is no longer plugged in is named as silent in the plan rather than quietly replaced by the default, so a take never records the wrong microphone by surprise.',
			'An input channel\'s plug list also ends with the House patch: the surface for the sources a track cannot own. The guitar on Input 1 or 2 is already a track input, but a voice and the deck are peer sources of the house plan, so their monitor, effects and recording live here. It lists every source in Sound\'s plan with its kind, the machine that binds it, and whether it is bound, silent or pending. A source routed into a bus has a Monitor slider for its own level, before its effects, and a Mute switch; both move the engine live, and the slider\'s position is saved with the plan. The effects row is that source\'s own chain: choose an engine effect from the list and press Add, press the box beside an effect to bypass it, or the cross to remove it. The chain is saved in the plan in its own order and the native engine builds it on the source before the mix. Record house mix records the chosen bus - the plan\'s mix bus first, then house, then the first bus - for the number of seconds shown, with the transport\'s tempo, signature and metronome volume as its click. The click is heard in the monitor and is never written into the file; Stop ends the recording and closes the WAV, and the section shows the file\'s path.', 
			'The Synth app is a channel input: open it, then set a channel\'s input to Synth and what you play records through that channel\'s tone stack and pedals.'
		],
		steps => [
			'Open Studio and add a track per instrument or take; rename it in its panel and press Delete twice to remove it.',
			'Record or drop a clip onto a track; drag its edges to trim and overlap to crossfade.',
			'Route a pedalboard onto a channel to shape its sound, then mix the tracks together.',
			'Add effects to a track from the pedalboard, reorder them in its chain strip, and use their footswitches to compare with and without; switch on a mastering effect under the strips when the whole mix needs it.',
			'Hide or float the channel strips, transport or tracks when the window is small; Dock brings a panel back to its packed place.',
			'Switch the Tracks bar to Select to draw a range, then Loop it, Bounce it through the tracks\' pedals, or Export it; right-click a take for Delete or Bounce.',
