$topic{'ide'} = {
		title => 'IDE', category => 'apps', app => 'ide',
		templates => [ 'templates/apps/coding_chat.html.ep', 'public/js/manager/coding.js' ],
		controls => [ 'Coding agent', 'Menu', 'New file', 'Save' ],
		summary => 'A colour-coded editor for Perl, JavaScript, HTML and templates.',
		verified => '2026-11-15',
		body => [
			'IDE edits code with syntax colouring for Perl, JavaScript, HTML and templates. It is what the project is developed in from inside itself.',
			'The code button in the toolbar opens the Coding agent\'s own chatbox: ask it what to change and it edits the repo, checks the change and ships it, reporting each step. It is the same agent the Guide\'s Coding toggle drives, and the editor keeps its own conversation. The + in its bar opens another agent, each with its own thread, and the bar drags the panel anywhere on the screen — it floats over the windows like the Guide\'s associate, and it reopens where you left it. A run keeps going in its own process: reloading the page or restarting the tree does not lose it, the panel picks the run back up when it returns, and stop in its bar asks it to finish the step it is on. Beside each step, thinking opens the model\'s working for that turn, the answer is written into the panel as it arrives, and the foot of the log says what the run is doing now. A task can also be handed to a subagent: it investigates or makes an edit inside the paths it is given, reports back, and never ships - the finishing routine stays with the agent you gave the task to. The subagent limit is set beside the Coding model in Configure → Plat → Coding: ten by default, and zero turns subagents off. A fix that has shipped reaches the page on its own: a stylesheet or template change lands in place, while a change to the manager\'s scripts or its shell asks once with a press-to-update note (a hidden tab reloads itself, and a chat in the panel survives it). Double-press the Assistant button for the Agents list: every coding conversation and guide associate, named, with history a press away — and the panels you left open come back with it.',
			'That chatbox also holds the error watch\'s staging area: press fixes in its corner to see every error caught in the running tree\'s log (switch the watch on under Configure → Plat → Self-healing), each with a fix the Coding agent has proposed. Apply writes the proposal, checks it compiles, and gives it the same finishing routine a chat change gets — bump, commit, push — and the panel\'s restart link puts it live. Forget throws a row away, and nothing is written until Apply. Every fix that ships is recorded: Fixed so far in Configure → Plat → Self-healing keeps the list, and review shows it.',
			'Only the top of the tree loads at first; a folder brings its own contents in when you unfold it, so a big project opens quickly. The tree reopens with the folders you had unfolded still open, the window returns to whichever pane — the list or the editor — you left it on, and the file list and each open file keep their place, so you come back to where you were.'
		],
		steps => [
			'Open the IDE and choose a file from the project tree; unfold a folder to see what is in it.',
			'Edit with syntax colouring and the code helpers.',
			'Save; a Perl or JavaScript change needs a restart to take effect, while a template change is live at once.',
			'Press the code button to open the Coding agent and describe a change in words. Its stop control asks it to finish the step it is on without dropping the run.',
			'Drag the panel by its bar to move it; the + in the bar opens another agent, with its own conversation.',
			'Press fixes in its corner to review errors caught in the log and apply a proposed fix.'
		],
		related => [ 'editor', 'terminal' ]
	};

$topic{'weather'} = {
		title => 'Weather', category => 'apps', app => 'weather',
		templates => [ 'templates/apps/weather.html.ep', 'templates/apps/weather/style.html.ep', 'templates/apps/weather/now.html.ep', 'templates/apps/weather/history.html.ep', 'templates/apps/weather/radar.html.ep', 'templates/apps/weather/accuracy.html.ep', 'templates/apps/weather/automations.html.ep', 'templates/apps/weather/map.html.ep', 'public/js/manager/weather.js', 'public/js/manager/weather_history.js', 'public/js/manager/weather_automation.js', 'public/js/manager/weather_map.js', 'public/js/manager/weather_radar.js', 'public/js/manager/weather_sources.js' ],
		controls => [ 'Choose the place the weather is read for', 'Place a new point on the map and save it as a place', 'Choose how this place reads its weather', 'Keep this place in the hourly weather pass', 'Ask the provider again instead of using the ten-minute cache', 'Read the weather here without keeping a place', 'Change the map under the weather layers', 'Play the radar frames in a loop', 'Drag to see the radar at another time' ],
		verified => '2026-11-24',
		summary => 'Current conditions and a seven-day forecast for the phone, tablet or machine you choose.',
		body => [
			'Weather reads the house\'s own GPS fixes, not a fixed city page. The picker lists places, not machines: machines reporting the same ground share one entry, and each entry is named after the place the weather service reads for (Kitchener rather than a coordinate), so the list reads as places. The location dropdown and Add stay at the top of the window while a tab scrolls, so the place you are reading stays in view. Hover an entry to see which machines are there. A saved place within thirty kilometres of a fix is where that reading belongs, so a nearby town does not become a second place. The house\'s home plate is its own station: it sits first in the picker, marked always checked, and the hourly pass always reads there, whatever phone is away. Moving the home plate to a new place unanchors the old home; it stays in Places as an added place, and Anchor keeps it recording if you want it. The app\'s display name follows the new home when it still reads as the old home\'s name; a name you set yourself is left alone. Choosing a place asks Environment Canada for the nearest city when it is in Canada, the US National Weather Service when it is in the United States, and Open-Meteo anywhere else. A Canadian fix therefore names the nearest Environment Canada city instead of an hour-away city page. A place outside Canada and the United States is named by a reverse geocoder (BigDataCloud), and the answer is kept, so the picker reads a place rather than a coordinate.',
			'The moon beside the condition icon is computed locally, not scraped: its phase, illuminated percentage and the lit limb are arithmetic, so a new house needs no web page and no key. The lit side is drawn on the right while waxing and mirrored for a southern place. The Moon app keeps the same reading as a daily measure, and the whole-house evaluation reads it as a condition. Below the current card, Space weather shows the planetary K index and its 24-hour peak, Dst, the north-south field (Bz), the solar-wind speed, the R/S/G storm scales, the strongest Kp forecast for the next three days, F10.7, the monthly sunspot number, the aurora probability over the house, and any active geomagnetic, radiation or radio-blackout alert. Each alert is an accordion like a weather statement: the bulletin\'s product line is the header, and the full message opens below it with its issue and valid-until times. A bulletin whose stated end has passed, or an open-ended alert issued more than a day ago, is not shown. A Local magnetic field line names the nearest reporting observatory, its distance, and the total field F, declination D and inclination I. The 900 KB aurora map is read for the one cell over the house and discarded; only the percentage is kept. It is fetched separately and cached, so an unreachable space-weather or observatory service leaves the forecast untouched; the same readings are recorded for the evaluation, and a backfill gives the geomagnetic history the report reads.' ,
			'The current card shows the temperature, condition, feels like, high and low, wind and gusts, humidity, pressure, dew point, visibility and UV. Below it are the next 24 hours, beginning with the hour ahead, which scroll sideways, and a fourteen-day forecast: the national service\'s week, then Open-Meteo\'s later days, with an alert banner when the provider has one; the banner uses the statement first line as the accordion title and reads the statement body from the Environment Canada report into that accordion, with the issue and expiry times and a link at the end to the full report. Each day reads as its weekday over a month/day/year stamp, and a day past this week says Next before the weekday. Tap a day to open its detail: the hours when precipitation is likely, a small temperature chart shaded where precipitation is likely, the warmest and coolest hours, and the day\'s sunrise, sunset, UV and wind; tap it again to close. The foot names Open-Meteo when later days came from it. The foot names the provider, the distance to the city when Environment Canada was used, and when the reading was fetched.',
			'Use browser location asks the browser for a one-off coordinate; it is not written to the GPS record. It reads the weather there without keeping a place. When you are away from every saved place, Check weather here keeps that reading and adds the place. Refresh asks the provider again instead of using the ten-minute cache. A device with no fresh fix says so and leaves the other machines in the picker.',
			'Add opens the same canvas map Travel uses, with its tile provider and measured maps. Tap the map to place the flag, tap again to move it, and Save adds that coordinate to Places; tick Keep recording here to anchor it. Cancel closes the map and returns to the view you had. Leave the name empty and the place is named after the weather service\'s nearest city, or by a reverse geocoder outside Canada and the United States, never a coordinate. The hourly pass does not follow the phone: it reads the home plate and the places you anchored, and Check weather here adds a place when you are away from all of them, so a visit is a place you can find later. Anchor, beside Delete, keeps the selected place in the hourly pass; press it again to stop checking it every hour. Delete removes the place you are looking at and tombstones its readings; press it twice. The same Delete sits beside At in a rule, and a rule that named the removed place reads as a deleted place until you choose another. The date beside a place is its latest fix, written month/day/year and hour:minute:second.',
			'The provider follows the coordinate. To force one, set the misc setting weather_provider to ec, nws or openmeteo; leave it empty for automatic. Sources, beside Delete, sets it for one place instead: Closest is the default and reads the barometer from the nearest Weather Underground station and the other readings from the next nearest, leaving the forecast with the usual provider; the other choices are Environment Canada, Open-Meteo, the US National Weather Service, or one named station. A personal station uses a free Weather Underground key: save your own in the same dialog, or let the app read the public key the Weather Underground page itself uses. Back closes the dialog and returns to the place you were reading. The Now card names the station that supplied the barometer and the one that supplied the other readings.',
			'The History tab reads the old weather appointments and the app\'s own readings back to the start of the house\'s records, folding them into one row per day with the high, low, mean, condition, wind and forecast humidity the old app logged. Each source is named for its place — Warwick Weather, Charlottetown Weather — so the list reads as places rather than app names. History source picks which of them to read; the app chooses the one whose city matches the forecast. The app\'s own source follows the place on screen: pick the phone and its days are the phone\'s, pick a saved place and they are that place\'s. Each place the station has readings for keeps its own source — Warwick Weather, Toronto Weather — so a home that moved keeps its old days under the old place instead of hiding them behind the new home. Readings the old apps kept never named a place, so they stay in every view. A place you delete is tombstoned with its readings, so History does not keep what you removed. Backfill history rebuilds that view. Choose 1 day (hourly) to read the last 24 hours as one temperature per hour instead of one row per day. Actual high and low are the extremes of the readings the house kept, so they are sampled rather than a daily extreme. Three more charts sit under the temperature: rainfall for the range, from Open-Meteo\'s gridded rainfall for the place on screen (with the daily chance of rain drawn as a line); snowfall for the same range, its gridded snowfall in centimetres; and the planetary Kp and Dst readings the space-weather pass keeps, one row a day.',
			'The Radar tab draws the place on screen with the same canvas map Travel uses, and its Map dropdown offers the same tile services, with the Travel app\'s own choice selected (the house street map falls back to OpenStreetMap in this panel). The row above the map switches the layers: Precipitation is RainViewer\'s rain radar, Clouds is NASA\'s daily cloud fraction, Temperature is NASA\'s land-surface temperature, Snow is its snow cover, Wildfire smoke is its aerosol layer (smoke and haze), Magnetic is NOAA\'s aurora probability oval, and Winds draws Open-Meteo wind arrows over the view; pan or zoom and the wind grid refills for what is on screen. Every layer is keyless. The radar, GIBS, aurora and wind answers are each cached for a while, so switching a layer off and on again does not download it twice. The slider under the map steps through the timeline: each computer and server records a five-by-five radar tile window around the home plate every ten minutes and keeps the hours set under Configure → Plat → Weather, so the past can run back a day; the live RainViewer frames cover the last two hours, and its nowcast is used when present. Beyond the radar, Open-Meteo\'s model rain is drawn as a heat field on a grid around the place, hourly, for the forecast hours set in the same place. Play loops the timeline; the label names the frame, and the note under it names the span the sources actually gave. RainViewer\'s free feed itself only publishes two hours, which is why the house records its own.',
			'The Accuracy tab compares each daily forecast the Weather app recorded, made one to seven days before the date, with what the house actually measured. The summary gives the mean absolute error for the high and low and the condition match for each lead day; the detail table shows the selected lead. Forecast snapshots begin when this app first loads, because the old app kept only seven raw pages, not its forecasts. Choose the history source that matches the forecast city for a fair comparison.',
			'The Automations tab keeps the rules that turn the weather into an app. A condition rule names a word the condition must contain, the app to start, and whether to start it or only record it. When the condition no longer contains the word, the app is stopped. The rain and snow rules from the old weather app are carried over as precipitation rules (the condition word decides whether it is rain or snow — flurries, sleet, freezing drizzle and the like included — and the nearest airport\'s METAR is read too: when it reports a precipitation type, that wins over the forecast word. The local gauge confirms that something is falling; temperature is used only when neither word names a type). They start and stop on the reading itself: a dry reading clears the app at once instead of waiting for the next check; the fog, sun and thunder rules stay condition rules. The old page keeps its hourly readings but stops driving apps, so the rules follow the house rather than a city an hour away. Each rule also names the place it is about: At lists Any location, the home plate, each place the house\'s machines are on (machines at one place share an entry) and the places you saved or anchored. A rule with a place acts only on a reading taken there, so rain on the phone and rain at the cottage are separate rules; the rules carried over from the old app are kept at the home place, and the tab shows the rules for the location chosen in the picker above, so each place has its own list. A rule that says Any location applies everywhere and stays in every view. A device entry is read when you press Check now; the home plate, anchored places and places a rule names are read every hour. The app field suggests the house\'s apps as you type and shows their names. The house server checks on its own about every hour at the home plate and each anchored or rule-named place, once for the whole household, and it is the machine that starts or stops an app, so one reading is one action however many screens show the weather. If the house server goes away, the other machines choose one of themselves to keep the pass and the rules running until it returns, and once the whole house is back together the app folds the duplicate readings that split made. On another machine Check now asks the provider immediately and keeps the reading for the location on screen; the house server applies the rules.',
			'Measurement automations read the values the app records: wind gust, wind speed, wind direction, pressure, precipitation rate, precipitation total, precipitation, temperature, feels like, humidity, visibility, dew point, UV and condition. Add conditions with a comparison (a gust above 20 km/h, a pressure below 101.2 kPa, a wind from the North), and all of them must be true. At works the same way as on a condition rule, so a cabin gust and a phone gust can be different rules. A rule can start an app, stop an app, or run a command. Give it a duration to require the condition to hold for minutes or hours; the window starts counting at the first reading the house keeps, so a six-hour rule begins six hours after that reading, and only readings from the rule\'s place count. A start rule also stops its app when the condition clears; tick Hold the stop until the next weather check to keep the app on through one dry reading and stop it at the next check, while a manual check stops it at once if the condition has cleared. While a start rule keeps its app running, each check keeps that day\'s reading on the app (one a day, updated as the day goes on), so the app\'s bottom line has the precipitation for today and this week; the rate reads as its peak and the total adds each day\'s reading, so a week is the rain that fell in it. A rule that finds its app already running with no memory of starting it adopts it, and stops it at the reading where the condition cleared. A run rule fires once each time the condition becomes true. Commands run on the house server, which applies the rules.',
		],
		steps => [
			'Open Weather from the start menu.',
			'Choose a place (This device, another place the house\'s machines are on, or a saved place).',
			'Read the current card, the moon beside the condition icon and the Space weather card; tap a day to open its hour-by-hour detail.',
			'Press Use browser location for a one-off coordinate, or Refresh to ask the provider again. Away from every saved place, Check weather here keeps a reading where you are.',
			'Press Add beside the location dropdown, tap the map to place the flag, leave the name empty to let the weather service name it, tick Keep recording here to anchor it, and Save. Press Anchor to keep a place in the hourly pass, or Delete to remove it and its readings.',
			'Press Sources beside Delete to choose how the selected place reads its weather. Closest is the default: the nearest station supplies the barometer, the next nearest the other readings, and the forecast stays with the usual provider.',
			'Open History to see the last eleven months of readings; choose 1 day (hourly) for the last 24 hours, or press Backfill history if the view is empty. Open Radar for its own tab: precipitation, clouds, temperature, snow, smoke, the aurora oval and wind arrows over the Travel map, with a timeline slider and Play under the map. Its archive and model forecast are set under Configure → Plat → Weather.',
			'Open Automations to see the rules for the location you have chosen. Add a condition rule or a measurement rule; it begins at that location, and At changes it. Press Check now to run the rules at once.',
			'Open Accuracy, choose a lead day and compare the forecast high, low and condition with the measured day.',
		],
		related => [ 'configure', 'travel' ]
	};

$topic{'power'} = {
		title => 'Power', category => 'apps', app => 'power',
		templates => [ 'templates/apps/power.html.ep', 'public/js/manager/power.js', 'public/js/manager/taskbar_power.js', 'templates/manager.html.ep', 'public/css/manager.css' ],
		controls => [ 'Stop the pack charging at this percentage', 'Let JawnOS write the CPU and sleep settings', 'Choose the plan while plugged in', 'Store the plan for mains power', 'Apply the matching plan when the cable changes', 'Use the saved plan for the current source now' ],
		verified => '2026-11-23',
		summary => 'Battery, CPU speed, the charge limit, saved power plans, sleep and display settings, and a low-battery guard for a computer or a controller.',
		body => [
			'Power reads this machine\'s battery and CPU and keeps one plan for each power source. The battery card shows the charge, whether it is charging, discharging or full, and the time left (or the time to full while it is charging), taken from the pack\'s own gauge where it reports one. A machine with no battery simply hides that card; a machine with no CPU speed control says so where the CPU figures would be.',
			'The two plan cards are On mains and On battery. Each has a power plan - Auto, Power saver, Balanced or Performance - and a CPU speed limit from 25% to 100%. Save stores that source\'s plan; when the machine is already on that source the plan is applied at once, and the other card waits for the day the cable changes. With Switch plans automatically on, the house applies the saved plan within about ten seconds of the source changing, so plugging in a laptop or pulling the cable from the Gou moves the CPU without anyone opening the app. When the machine has just booted, the saved plan is taken back once even with that switch off, because the kernel forgets its governor at boot and a reboot is not a plan switch; that restore happens only while JawnOS manages power.',
			'A plan is a governor choice, an energy preference and a turbo choice, tried in an order that fits the kernel: Power saver prefers powersave, asks for the power energy preference and turns turbo off; Balanced prefers schedutil, asks for balance_performance and leaves turbo on; Performance prefers performance. Auto writes nothing and leaves the kernel - or auto-cpufreq, when it is installed - in charge. Choosing any other plan also stops a competing power daemon - power-profiles-daemon, TLP or auto-cpufreq - because two managers re-applying their own profile would race; with Auto they are left alone. The daemon is only stopped, not disabled, so a reboot starts it again. The CPU speed limit caps the clock inside whatever governor is chosen, so it works on kernels that offer only one governor. The CPU panel shows the governor, energy preference, driver, number of clock domains, the current and maximum speed in GHz, and the hardware maximum, after each change.',
			'Above the plans, Power says who is managing the power right now: JawnOS and the plan it applied, or a competing daemon - auto-cpufreq, TLP or power-profiles-daemon - that is still running. Auto leaves such a daemon in charge; choosing another plan stops it so the two are not racing. While a real plan is in effect, the power watch checks again and stops a daemon that has started by itself. On a machine JawnOS can manage, a JawnOS manages power checkbox sits beside that line: ticked, JawnOS writes the CPU and sleep settings and takes the saved plan and the saved charge limit back at each boot and each start; unticked, the system is left in charge, the plan controls wait, and nothing is applied until it is ticked again. JawnOS writes down the daemon it stopped and whether that daemon was enabled, so unticking the box or quitting JawnOS puts it back, and a crash leaves the note for the next start to restore when JawnOS is not managing. A phone shows no checkbox, because Android gives an app no way to change its power settings.',
			'When the pack reports a charge ceiling - the laptops that stop charging at a chosen percentage - the battery card carries a Charge limit slider from 50% to 100%. Moving it writes the battery\'s own charge_control_end_threshold, so the pack stops there even when the cable stays in. JawnOS remembers the chosen percentage and puts it back after a start or a boot, and whenever a competing daemon or the firmware has returned the pack to 100%, so the ceiling survives a restart; a battery without that attribute hides the row instead of offering a control that writes nowhere.',
			'A computer or a controller is guarded on its own. With no cable and the warning threshold reached (ten percent by default), a Battery low notification warns the person to plug it in; at the shutdown threshold (five percent by default), confirmed by the next ten-second power-watch check, JawnOS asks for a clean Shut down, so the pack\'s own protection does not cut the rail in the middle of a write. Only a computer or a controller does this: a phone keeps Android\'s own battery handling. The battery card carries a Shut down before the battery dies switch and two percentages for it - when it warns and when it shuts down, 10% and 5% by default; untick the switch to leave the machine to its own daemon. Its percentage is a resting-voltage estimate, and the charger holds the terminal near 4.2 V, so the panel keeps the last reading from before the cable went in - marked last reading before charging - until the PMIC reports the pack full.',
			'On the JawnOS live image and the desktop app the power files are the same Linux sysfs the rest of the house uses; the live image\'s passwordless sudo is used when the files are root-owned. On the Gou the same path covers its battery and CPU. On a phone, the battery comes from the app\'s own battery bridge; Android gives an app no way to change the CPU governor, so the CPU panel says the plan is not available there rather than pretending it changed. A machine that can power itself also shows System power here - Restart, Shut down and Sleep - the same fixed actions the Leave menu offers; a phone shows none of them, because it cannot power itself.',
			'When the taskbar is showing, a Power widget can ride on it: a small pack glyph, the percentage and the time left or to full. On a machine with no battery it shows the plan in use for mains power instead of a percentage. A press opens a small panel with the battery summary and the power profile for the source the machine is on - Auto, Power saver, Balanced or Performance - so the profile can be changed without opening the app; Open Power at the foot opens this window. The panel writes the source the machine is on at the moment of the press, not the source it last read. It also carries that source\'s CPU speed limit as a − / + stepper in 5% steps from 25% to 100%, applied as soon as it changes, so Performance can keep a custom cap without opening the app; a plan press keeps the saved limit. Add items -> Widgets puts it on the bar or takes it off, its right-click menu chooses Icon, Icon and status, or Disabled, and edit mode moves it like the clock or the window list.',
			'Sleep and display is the card below the plans. Its Power profile selector names the profile in effect, and the four controls below it belong to that profile: each of Auto, Power saver, Balanced and Performance keeps its own timers and lid action, so switching the plan switches them too. Screen off after chooses 1, 2, 5, 10, 15 or 30 idle minutes, or Never; it is set through the desktop\'s gsettings where that exists, or xset on an X display, and a machine with neither says the screen timer is not available. On a controller the timer is the panel\'s own: the backlight goes dark and nothing is stopped, so the first press brings the light back, and the Configuration page\'s Power tile opens this same page. Sleep after chooses 1, 2, 5, 10, 15, 30 or 60 idle minutes, or Never, and is written to a logind drop-in together with the lid action. When the lid closes appears only on a machine with a real lid: Suspend, Hibernate, Hybrid suspend, Shut down or Do nothing. Sleep type offers only the states this machine reports - Suspend, Hibernate, Hybrid suspend and Suspend then hibernate - and Sleep now enters the chosen state at once. A profile that is not the one the machine is on is only stored; its timers take effect when that plan does.',
		],
		steps => [
			'Open Power from the start menu, or press the Power widget in the taskbar and then Open Power in its panel.',
			'Read the battery card: charge, state and the time left or to full.',
			'If the battery offers a Charge limit, drag the slider to the percentage it should stop at; the pack keeps that ceiling when plugged in and across restarts.',
			'Tick JawnOS manages power beside the management line to let JawnOS write the CPU and sleep settings; untick it and the system is left in charge, the plan controls wait, and nothing is applied until it is ticked again. A phone has no such checkbox.',
			'On On mains, choose the plan and the CPU speed limit the machine should use while plugged in, then press Save.',
			'On On battery, choose the plan and limit for battery use, then press Save.',
			'Leave Switch plans automatically on to apply the matching plan when the cable changes; turn it off to switch only when you press Apply now.',
			'Press Apply now to use the current source\'s saved plan immediately.',
			'On a machine that can power itself, press Restart, Shut down or Sleep in System power; a phone has no such row.',
			'Read the CPU panel to see the governor, energy preference, driver and clock speed after a change.',
			'In Sleep and display, choose the Power profile to edit, then when the screen turns off and when the system sleeps, then press Save; each profile keeps its own timers and lid action.',
			'If the machine has a lid, choose what closing it does; a machine without a lid has no such row.',
			'Choose a Sleep type this machine offers and press Sleep now to enter that state immediately.',
		],
		prompt => 'The reader is using JawnOS\'s Power app. It has one battery card, two plan cards (On mains and On battery), an automatic-switch tick, a Sleep and display card and a CPU panel. A plan is Auto, Power saver, Balanced or Performance, and each source also has a CPU speed limit. Saving the source the machine is on applies it at once; the other source is stored until the cable changes. Sleep and display holds a Power profile selector plus the screen timeout, the sleep timeout, the lid-close action and the sleep type for that profile; each profile keeps its own block; the lid row exists only on a machine with a lid, and the sleep type offers only states this machine reports. On a computer or a controller the low-battery guard warns and then shuts down cleanly at two percentages on the battery card (10% and 5% by default); the switch turns it off, and a phone keeps its own battery handling. Never claim a CPU speed, a screen timer or a sleep state changed on a machine where the panel says it is unavailable, and never describe a phone as changing a performance mode its app does not offer. If unsure, describe the control\'s label and what the panel reports rather than inventing a sequence.',
		related => [ 'taskbar', 'start_menu', 'configure' ]
	};

$topic{'notifications'} = {
		title => 'Notifications', category => 'apps', app => 'notifications',
		templates => [ 'templates/apps/notifications.html.ep', 'templates/configure/app_notification.html.ep', 'public/js/manager/notifications.js', 'public/js/manager/notifications_toast.js', 'public/js/manager/taskbar_notifications.js', 'templates/notification.html.ep' ],
		controls => [ 'Type to filter the list', 'Show only one app in the list', 'Clear every notice the filter shows', 'Remove this notice', 'Choose the budget statuses that announce', 'Choose the budget types that announce', 'Choose the devices that play this appointment\'s notification sound', 'Type an appointment name and press Enter to let it announce notifications', 'Stop this appointment from announcing notifications' ],
		verified => '2026-11-16',
		summary => 'The house\'s notices, with a search and an app filter, and the taskbar bell.',
		body => [
			'Notifications lists what the house has told you, newest first: a payment, an alarm, a request to pair, a plan that was applied, and anything else an app announces. Each row names its app and its time, and its X removes that notice. Press a row to open that app\'s appointment window.',
			'The search box filters the list as you type, and the app picker narrows it to one app. Remove all clears the filtered list.',
			'Two views share the window. Main is the list above. Appointments lists every appointment that announces notifications on any device, each as an accordion that opens to the same panel Configure shows: its alarm, its sound and text for default, start, stop and budget, two pickers for the budget statuses and budget types that may announce, and Chime on. Chime on is a multiselect of every machine the house knows by signatorial, the device types Computer, Mobile, Server and Controller, plus Every device; only the chosen machines play the sound and post the phone notification, while the notice still appears in this list. The input at the top adds a public or dangerous appointment; private appointments are never offered. Turn off notifications stops an appointment on every device that was announcing it, without opening Configure.',
			'A budget notice is a card, not a stream: while the appointments behind the reading have not changed, a moved number rewrites the same card instead of arriving again. A new appointment or a new budget period is a new notice.',
			'A notice also rises as a small card in the top-right corner of the desk while you work, with its title and line. Press the card to open the app it came from, press its X to dismiss it, or let it fade after a few seconds; hovering a card holds it. This is the house\'s own card, not the browser\'s notification, so it follows the theme and can always be seen.',
			'The taskbar can carry a Notifications widget: a bell with a count of what has arrived since the list was last opened. A press opens a small panel with the newest notices and a search box; press a notice there to open its appointment window, and Open Notifications at the foot opens this app. The count clears when the panel or the app is opened, and it is kept per browser. Add items -> Widgets puts the bell on the bar or takes it off, its right-click menu chooses Icon, Icon and status, or Disabled.',
		],
		steps => [
			'Open Notifications from the start menu, or press the bell widget in the taskbar and then Open Notifications.',
			'Type in the search box to narrow the list; choose an app to show only its notices.',
			'Switch to Appointments to see and change what announces; type an appointment into the add box and press Enter to let it announce, or press Turn off notifications to stop one.',
			'Press a notice, or a row in the bell\'s panel, to open that app\'s appointment window; press its X to remove it, or Remove all to clear the filtered list.',
			'Press a rising notice card to open the app it came from, or its X to dismiss it.',
			'Add the Notifications widget under the taskbar\'s Add items -> Widgets; its count clears when you open the list.',
		],
		related => [ 'taskbar', 'configure', 'clock' ]
	};

$topic{'sound'} = {
		title => 'Sound', category => 'apps', app => 'sound',
		templates => [ 'templates/apps/sound.html.ep', 'public/js/manager/sound.js', 'public/js/manager/sound/03_apps.js', 'public/js/manager/taskbar_sound.js', 'public/icons/sound.svg', 'templates/start_menu/start_menu.html.ep', 'templates/manager.html.ep', 'public/css/manager.css' ],
		controls => [ 'Set the house level: Sound, Quiet, Mute or Silence', 'Make this output the default speaker', 'Drag or scroll to set the default speaker volume', 'Mute the default speaker', 'Make this input the default microphone', 'Drag or scroll to set the default microphone volume, when the mixer exposes it', 'Mute the default microphone', 'Let the audio engine own this machine\'s inputs and outputs', 'device input channel', 'device output channel', 'The machine this source runs on; leave blank for this machine', 'The machine this sink plays on; leave blank for this machine', 'where the samples come from', 'where they go', 'Save the plan for the whole house', 'Choose the audio interface the engine opens', 'Use this device and restart the engine', 'The address this machine listens on for a peer\'s audio; leave it empty for off', 'Save the uplink and restart the engine', 'Show this section' ],
		verified => '2026-11-22',
		summary => 'Output and input devices, the house channels, app levels, live playback and recording streams, and the machine\'s volume and mute.',
		body => [
			'Sound lists the machine\'s output devices and input devices. The window is four pills - Patchbay, IO, Profiles and Apps - and shows one section at a time; the pills scroll sideways on a narrow window, and the section a person leaves is the one that opens next time on this device. IO holds the output and input lists and the media devices this browser uses; Profiles holds the house Level and the per-app profiles; Patchbay is the routing table; Apps is the live mixer for the house channels, the app levels and every stream the sound system is carrying. The default speaker and the default microphone are marked Default; every other row carries Set default. The default output has a volume slider and a Mute button, and the default microphone has the same controls when the machine\'s mixer exposes them. Every volume slider in the window answers to the wheel as well as to the drag: one notch moves it one step - a point for a volume, one level for an app - and a two-finger swipe on a trackpad does the same, so the section does not scroll under the pointer. The list is read from the machine itself - pactl, then wpctl, then amixer - so the ids on the screen are the devices the controls actually write to.',
			'The Speaker and Microphone lines above each list name the device this browser uses, read from the browser\'s own media-device list: the same enumerateDevices() source the keyboard\'s walkboy panel reads its A.V. list from. That label need not be the server\'s default, because a browser and the system mixer can choose differently; the Default badge follows the system mixer, which is what Set default changes.',
			'On a phone the app uses the app\'s own audio volume bridge: the streams are listed as outputs and the media stream is the default. The bridge has no input streams and no mute setter, so the microphone section says it is not available there and the Mute button stays disabled. A machine with no mixer at all says no devices were found instead of pretending to control one.',
			'When the taskbar is showing, a Sound widget can ride on it: a speaker glyph, the volume percentage and the mute state. A press opens a quick panel with an output slider and an input slider, a Mute button for each, and every output and input listed so one press makes it the default; Open Sound at the foot opens this app. A scroll wheel over the widget turns the default output a gentle step a notch and posts once the scroll settles; the same wheel works over the output and input sliders while the panel is open. Add items -> Widgets puts it on the bar or takes it off, its right-click menu chooses Icon, Icon and status, or Disabled, and edit mode moves it like the clock or the window list. It reads /manager/sound_status twice a minute.',
			'Media devices is the browser picker: it lists the inputs and outputs this browser can use and lets one device of each kind be on. A recording or a call takes its microphone, camera and speaker from those picks, which is the same choice jp.js reads.',
			'The house level and the app profiles are a policy over one mixer, and Patchbay, the first section, is that mixer\'s table. It has three kinds of thing: a source makes samples (an interface input, a music player, a phone, the reception handset), a bus mixes them (a Studio track, the click, a monitor mix, the house bus), and a sink plays them (the interface outputs, this machine\'s speakers, a zone, another machine). A route is one edge with a gain, a pan and a mute. Add a source or a sink with its name, kind, device channel and, when it lives on another machine, that machine\'s name - blank means this machine, and the engine reports the nodes it cannot bind as silent. A bus needs only a name. Then add routes between them and press Save plan; the plan belongs to the house, so the same table is what every machine reads.',
			'The engine owns this machine\'s audio is the master switch. Engine device chooses which interface the engine opens: each card on this machine is listed as ALSA, then, for a card the sound server knows, the PipeWire profiles that card offers such as Pro Audio or Analog Stereo, then PipeWire default and null. The picker remembers a card by its name rather than its number, because the operating system can number the same interface differently after a restart; the card you chose stays the card that opens. Choosing a PipeWire profile sets that profile on the card, moves the default output to the sink the profile names, clears any plan input whose node the profile renamed, and restarts the engine, so Pro Audio is picked here instead of in pavucontrol, and the note beside the picker says when the card is on a different profile from the saved one. Use this device saves the choice and restarts the engine, so a second interface, or the same card on another profile, can be picked without a terminal. A machine with no sound server lists its cards for ALSA only. On, the audio engine takes this machine\'s inputs and outputs and plays the plan; the line beside the switch says the engine\'s state, how many frames it has rendered, how many xruns it has seen, its rate and period, and how many nodes are silent here. Ordinary audio does not stop: Music, a video, a call and the house\'s notifications are captured from a system mix and played through the engine too, so the machine is not left silent while it owns the output. A route\'s gain and pan sliders drive the running patch as you drag them - a muted route sends nothing - while Save plan still writes the plan, so a live trim is only the running patch and is not kept. Off, the previous default output comes back, the system mix is removed, and the machine goes back to its ordinary PipeWire and pavucontrol behaviour; the plan is kept for the day it is turned on again. If the mix\'s own record was lost - the app died while it owned the output - Off still finds the mix by its sink, puts the output and its volume back, and the note under the switch says the mix was found without its record. A machine with no engine, or one whose engine was not built, saves and shows the plan and says the engine is not running; it does not lose the table.',
			'A source\'s kind says where its samples come from. A handset source is the screened call: the reception mixer publishes the caller on jawn_call_in, so a call the receptionist answers routes like any other input. A device source is a channel on the audio interface the engine owns. A peer source is another machine\'s input, carried to this machine over the Uplink. A peer source row also carries Peer latency, a number of milliseconds from 0 to 2000: how late that peer\'s audio arrives, so a positive value places its stream that much earlier. Beside it, Peer buffer is how far behind the clock the master holds that peer\'s audio before reading it, from 85 to 2000 milliseconds: keep it at 85 for a machine on the same wired network, and raise it for a peer on a slow or uneven path, such as the T-Deck on Wi-Fi, so a late datagram is placed instead of covered with silence. The engine never reads with less than one jitter window (about 85 ms) and reports the buffer it actually ran. Both are plan facts, not live trims: the engine reads them when it patches the peer source, so they take effect when Save plan patches a running engine and otherwise on the next patch or restart, not while a number is typed. While the engine owns the card, ordinary operating-system and app audio - Music, a video, a notification, and audio received from another machine - is carried by the engine too, through the machine\'s system mix, so nothing is lost to the switch. During a take or Listen, that system audio is heard on the monitor only: it is never written into the take. Turning the switch off puts back the output that was the default before the engine took the card.',
			'Uplink is the address this machine listens on for a peer\'s audio, written as host:port, for example 0.0.0.0:7060. Leave it blank for off. It takes effect only when the plan also has a peer source on this machine: the engine then answers the peer\'s handshake and mixes what it sends as that source. The house generates a session key once and keeps it, so the same key signs every session; it is never shown here or written into a page. Now Me carries that address, the source\'s stream number and the key to the T-Deck, which streams its microphone live while you record; the engine expands the deck\'s 16 kHz capture to its 48 kHz timeline before placing it, so the take lines up with the interface. Name the peer source after the deck, or leave a single peer on this machine, so Now Me knows which stream is the deck\'s. If the source runs on another House machine, its Machine field names that machine, and the deck dials that address (or the machine it already reaches when none is named). The line beside the field says what the engine reports - off, idle, running or an error - and whether the patch treats the peer as off, pending or capturing. A peer source on another machine is still silent here, and a take is never cut when the uplink changes.',
			'A node the plan names but this machine cannot bind - a phone, a peer on another machine, the reception handset or a zone - stays in the plan and is silent here, because the plan is one document for the whole house and the machine that holds the interface is the one that plays it. A peer source on this machine is the exception: with an Uplink it binds and mixes like any other input. Route delays are not implemented yet: a plan with one is refused with a message naming the route rather than applied with the delay quietly missing.',
			'The Apps pill is one live mixer for everything the machine plays and records, in four groups. House channels is Music, Phone and Alarm, each with a volume slider and a mute; these are the launcher\'s own gains, so a muted channel stays silent even on a machine with no card. JawnOS apps lists only the apps that have a window open on this machine right now, and each row\'s slider sets that app\'s level: Passive inherits the house level, Sound is normal, Quiet softens the non-essential sounds, Mute silences media and speech but keeps alarms and a ringing call, and Silence takes everything. An app with a window open but no level yet reads as Passive. When no window is open, or no browser is connected, the group says so instead of listing the whole catalogue. The app list and the two stream lists scroll when there are more rows than fit, with a keyboard-focusable scroll area. Playing now is one row for every playback stream the sound system reports - a browser window, a player, another program - showing its app, media name, program and output sink, with a volume slider and a mute. Recording now is the same for every stream using a microphone, showing its app, media name and input source. The rows refresh every two seconds while the Apps pill is open, so a stream that starts or ends appears without reopening the window, and the refresh stops when the pill is left.',
			'The sound system names a browser\'s streams by the media they are playing, not by tab: Chromium and Firefox appear as the window or media they carry, and the operating system exposes no finer per-tab control. A stream keeps the id the sound system gave it for as long as it plays, so a slider moves that stream alone. A machine with no pactl, or the stock pactl without JSON, simply lists no streams instead of reporting a fault.',
		],
		steps => [
			'Open Sound from the start menu, or press the speaker widget in the taskbar for its panel and then Open Sound there.',
			'Read the Output list; the row marked Default is the speaker the machine is using.',
			'Press a pill to move between Patchbay, IO, Profiles and Apps; the window opens on the one this device used last.',
			'In Apps, drag a house channel\'s slider or press Mute to mix Music, Phone and Alarm, and drag an app\'s slider to set that app\'s level.',
			'Scroll over a slider - the default output\'s or input\'s, or any row under Apps - to move it a notch at a time; the section stays still rather than scrolling under the pointer.',
			'To turn one browser window, player or program down, open Apps, find its row under Playing now or Recording now, and drag the slider or press Mute; the row names its app, media and output or input.',
			'Under Media devices, press a microphone, camera or speaker to make it the one recordings and calls use.',
			'In the widget\'s panel, drag the output or input slider, press Mute, or press a device row to make it the default.',
			'Scroll over the speaker widget to turn the output up or down.',
			'In Patchbay, add the sources and sinks this machine has - naming any that live on another machine - join them with a bus, and press Save plan; turn on The engine owns this machine\'s audio to hear it. Drag a route\'s gain or pan to trim the running patch.',
			'If the machine has more than one interface, choose it under Engine device and press Use this device; the engine restarts on the new card with the same plan. To play through the sound server on one profile of a card, choose the PipeWire entry named for that profile instead of the card\'s ALSA entry; the engine then plays out that profile\'s sink and the card keeps the profile.',
			'To receive another machine\'s audio, add a peer source to the plan, type the address this machine should listen on under Uplink (for example 0.0.0.0:7060) and press Save uplink; leave the field blank to turn it off. The line beside it says whether the engine has the uplink and how the peer is being mixed.',
			'If that peer\'s audio arrives late, set Peer latency on its source row in milliseconds, from 0 to 2000, then press Save plan; the engine reads the declaration when it patches the peer source, so a running engine picks it up at that save. If the take is still mostly silence, raise Peer buffer on the same row, from 85 to 2000 milliseconds, until the engine reports few late datagrams and little concealed audio.',
			'Press Set default on another output to move the default speaker there.',
			'Drag the Volume slider or press Mute to change the default output.',
			'Read the Input list for the default microphone; Set default moves it, and Volume and Mute work when the mixer exposes them.',
			'Read the Speaker and Microphone lines to see which devices this browser uses.',
			'Add or remove the Sound widget under the taskbar\'s Add items -> Widgets.',
		],
		prompt => 'The reader is using JawnOS\'s Sound app. It has four pills - Patchbay, IO, Profiles and Apps - and shows one section at a time. IO has an Output list and an Input list, each marking its Default device, a volume slider and a Mute button for the default output (and for the default microphone when the mixer exposes it), and Set default buttons on the other rows. The Speaker and Microphone lines name the browser\'s own devices, which may differ from the system default. Apps mixes the house channels (Music, Phone, Alarm), the levels of apps that have a window open on this machine, and every live playback and recording stream, each with a slider and a mute; the app and stream lists scroll when there are more rows than fit, and a volume slider also moves a step per wheel notch or trackpad swipe - the section does not scroll under the pointer. When no window is open, JawnOS apps says so rather than listing the whole catalogue. A browser is listed by the media it is playing, not by tab. The keyboard\'s walkboy panel remains the media picker. Never claim a device, channel, app level or stream changed when the app reports no devices or a disabled control; describe the row and the control instead.',
		related => [ 'taskbar', 'start_menu', 'clock' ]
	};

# The controller roster: the window that lists the controllers allowed to talk
# to the house, adds one and confirms its code. Storage and ROMs are named as
# the next slice, not built here.
$topic{'controllers'} = {
			title => 'Controllers', category => 'apps', app => 'controllers',
			templates => [ 'templates/controllers/controllers.html.ep', 'templates/controllers/style.html.ep', 'templates/controllers/body.html.ep', 'public/js/manager/controllers.js' ],
		controls => [ 'Put the house on a blank board', 'Keep the card or format it on first start', 'Name the controller for the network', 'Add the controller and read its code', 'Confirm the code the controller shows', 'Step through the storage setup' ],
			verified => '2026-11-24',
			summary => 'The controllers that can talk to the house: the roster, adding one and confirming its code.',
			body => [
				'Controller lists the controllers the house knows. Each row names the controller, where it was last seen (its hostname and address) and when, and a controller waiting to be confirmed is marked pending.',
				'To add one, type its name and press Add a controller. The house answers with a code, shown large on the page; type that same code on the controller, then type it under Approve a code and press Approve. The roster reads itself again once the code is accepted, and a refused code says why.',
				'Storage names where the controller keeps its documents, downloads, recordings, games, scans, pictures, videos and music: a folder on its SD card. A blank folder means nothing is merged, and the folders only merge while the card is mounted, so a controller can hold its own files without them joining the house. Walk me through it lists the same steps in order, ticking each one as it is done: put the card in this computer and mount it, type the folder it is mounted at, press Save to make its folders, then put games in Games. Each step carries the button that does it, and the Folders grid says which are on the card now. The Backups folder holds sealed snapshots from this machine, and Backup space caps the share of the card they may use.',
				'ROMs shows the controller\'s games two ways, on its own storage and on the SD card, and copies a game from one to the other. Select a controller first to say which one the page is showing.',
				'On a computer, New puts the house on a fresh controller. The field above the button gives the board its Name (it answers to that name on the network). The Wi-Fi it joins is the network this computer is on: the Network app already knows that connection and its key, so there is nothing to type, and a computer with no Wi-Fi flashes the board with no network. The card field says what to do with the card already in the board when it first starts: keep what is on it, or format it. New then looks for a board waiting in recovery mode; if there is none it shows how to wake one, and Scan looks again. When a board answers it writes the image, checks what it wrote, and fills in that name, that network and a house token before the board first starts; a flashed board is also given its own configuration, database and identity, so it starts as itself rather than as the card it was copied from. When the house domain is set, the join also names the board under it - the new name is added to the list in Configure -> Plat -> DNS - and gives it a certificate for that name; the house machine that holds the CA signs it, even when another computer flashed the board. While the image is written the panel shows how much is done and how fast, with a Cancel button beside it. The write runs on its own, so a panel restart or a reopened Controllers window picks the same flash up where it is; Cancel in the meantime leaves the board with no complete image, which the next flash replaces. The board also keeps a copy of the recovery image, so a card can be made recovery-ready again. The flash also writes the board\'s own way back onto its boot partition, so a board that will not start can be recovered without a microSD: run gou_recovery.pl on the board to arm it, and it comes back up exporting its disk over USB. When the write and its check finish the board restarts into JawnOS by itself; a board whose recovery firmware predates this cannot, and the panel says to press F3+F6 on it.',
				'The first time it starts, a flashed board finds its SD card and redeems that token. An ext4 card is adopted as it stands and given its folders, and a card holding somebody else\'s filesystem is left alone until someone decides. What the slot held is reported in the controller\'s Storage section: a card with another filesystem offers Adopt the card or Format the card, and a card with no filesystem offers Format the card, which asks once more because everything on it is erased. The choice made here is applied on that first start; a choice made later on this page reaches the board as a setting and is applied the next time the controller starts. A redeemed token files the board on the roster here with the same grant a citizen\'s own machine carries, so it starts syncing without a code; a controller holds that automatic sync until its card is mounted, so a board with no card, or a card that is not ready, does not pull the house\'s appointments and files onto its eMMC. It also files this house on the board\'s own roster. The code above is for a board that joins a different house.',
				'On the board, Configuration -> Power opens the controller\'s own Power page - the CPU plan, and the screen and suspend timeouts - rather than the desk, which a handheld has nowhere to draw.',
			],
			related => [ 'embedded', 'configure' ]
		};

$topic{'packages'} = {
	title => 'Packages', category => 'apps', app => 'packages',
	templates => [ 'templates/apps/packages.html.ep', 'public/js/manager/packages.js' ],
	controls => [ 'Search for a package', 'Read the installed and outdated lists again', 'Refresh the index without upgrading anything', 'Upgrade every outdated package', 'Install one package by name', 'Install a package file from this machine', 'Choose which package manager to use' ],
	verified => '2026-11-18',
	summary => 'The software on this machine: what is installed, what is outdated, and the changes the house may make.',
	body => [
		'Packages reads the software on the machine JawnOS is running on and, on a computer or server, changes it. It does not assume a manager: the page names the one it found and its version. A Debian or Ubuntu machine answers apt and dpkg; an Arch machine answers pacman, and the header also names yay or paru when one is installed for the AUR. Flatpak, Snap, pip3 and npm are used too when the machine has them, and each one lists, searches, installs, removes, purges and upgrades its own packages.',
		'When more than one manager is present, a Package manager selector under the header chooses which one the lists and buttons act on. The selector is absent on a machine with a single manager, where there is nothing to choose.',
		'The header names the manager and its version, and the line under it says what is happening now. Installed lists every package the manager knows, with its version and, under apt, its installed size; Outdated lists only the packages that have a newer version, old and new together. Both lists are capped - 2,000 rows for the installed list and 100 for a search - and say so when the cap is reached rather than silently stopping.',
		'Search asks the manager for one package name and lists what matches, each row with an Install button. Install takes one package name and installs it. Remove on a row removes the package but leaves its configuration files in place; Purge is the separate button that removes the package and the configuration too. Update index refreshes what the manager knows about available versions but upgrades nothing; a manager that keeps its own index says there is nothing to refresh. Upgrade all upgrades every outdated package. Install from file takes a .deb on apt or an Arch package (.pkg.tar.zst or .xz) on pacman, and refuses any other kind of file.',
		'Every change is confirmed first, and it runs only inside the JawnOS desktop window: a plain browser can read the lists but cannot install, remove or purge. Where root is needed the app runs the manager through sudo -n, which never asks for a password: if the machine will not allow that, the app says the action needs root and was refused instead of waiting for a prompt that cannot appear. The AUR helpers and the per-user managers (flatpak, pip, npm) are not wrapped in sudo, because they handle privilege themselves. Only one package operation runs at a time, so two clicks or two browsers cannot interleave an upgrade with a removal. After a change that touched the running system, a line says a restart is needed; it is the machine\'s cue to reboot, not something the app does.',
		'On a phone the app is read-only and says plainly why: the device runs a prebuilt runtime with no package manager, and the software it ships is updated together with the app. Installed lists the binaries that runtime ships, and there are no buttons for search, update, upgrade, install, remove or purge. The note also explains that developer tools can be added in a terminal on the device, outside this window. On a computer with no manager at all, the app also stays read-only and says so.',
		'On a machine whose games need a newer OpenSSL than its packages ship - the ODROID Go Ultra is one - a small private library layer may be installed under /opt for RetroArch. The page names that directory and the openssl version the package manager has, in one quiet line under the header, and says that upgrading openssl replaces the layer. Upgrade all installs the distribution\'s libraries system-wide and removes the private layer in the same run; if the upgrade fails, the layer and its loader file stay exactly as they were. The line is a note, not a button: there is nothing to press to remove it by hand.',
		'Packages does not build packages from source. pip upgrades its outdated packages one at a time, and a name that npm scopes (for example @scope/package) is accepted as one name. Remove leaves configuration in place; Purge is the action that deletes it.',
	],
	steps => [
		'Open Packages from the start menu.',
		'Read the header for the manager and its version; a phone says this runtime and offers no changes.',
		'If a Package manager selector is shown, choose the manager you want the lists and buttons to act on.',
		'Read Installed for the packages on the machine and Outdated for the ones with a newer version.',
		'Press Update index to refresh what is available; it upgrades nothing.',
		'Press Upgrade all to upgrade every outdated package, and confirm when asked.',
		'Type a package name in the search box and press Search, then press Install on a result.',
		'Type one package name and press Install to install it directly; press Remove on an installed row to take the package away, or Purge to take its configuration too.',
		'Choose a .deb or Arch package with the file field and press Install from file; any other file is refused.',
		'If a restart line appears, restart the machine when it suits you so the running system uses the new packages.',
	],
	prompt => 'The reader is using JawnOS\'s Packages app. It names the manager it detected (apt on Debian or Ubuntu, pacman on Arch, yay or paru for the AUR, or flatpak, snap, pip or npm when present, or this runtime on a phone with no manager) and its version. When several managers exist, a selector chooses which one the lists and buttons use. Installed and Outdated are read-only lists; Search, Update index, Upgrade all, Install, Remove, Purge and Install from file are the changes, and each change is confirmed, runs only in the JawnOS desktop window, and uses sudo -n only where root is needed. A quiet line under the header names a private OpenSSL layer for RetroArch, its directory and the installed openssl version, and says an upgrade replaces it. Never promise a change on a phone or a machine the page calls read-only, never claim a restart was performed (the line only says one is needed), and describe a refused sudo as refused rather than as a prompt. Remove keeps configuration; Purge deletes it. The app does not build from source.',
	related => [ 'start_menu', 'configure', 'terminal' ]
	};

1;
